{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-107937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-107937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-107937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19bcc4ffc2aa1140b1e57307a09982eb847f0e0440c73246c71cb4d3a58c9ce9"} {"question" "q-107937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-107937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-107937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}-16y=0,\\,y(1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=8{y}^{′}+16y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=8{x}_{2}+16{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=3,q=1,f(t)=16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q)\\cdot f(t) & =(3-1)(16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2(8)(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19bcc4ffc2aa1140b1e57307a09982eb847f0e0440c73246c71cb4d3a58c9ce9"} {"question" "q-109635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})"] ["math" "16({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})"] ["math" "4(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})"] ["math" "6({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})"] ["math" "8(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=10{y}^{′}-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=10{x}_{2}-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-1,q=2,f(t)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(-1-2)(-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3(-5)(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19bcc4ffc2aa1140b1e57307a09982eb847f0e0440c73246c71cb4d3a58c9ce9"} {"question" "q-109636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12(3{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2})"] ["math" "-15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2})"] ["math" "10(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2})"] ["math" "12(3{x}_{1}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2})"] ["math" "15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=4\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(0) & ={x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{y}^{″}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{x}_{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=-1,r=4,f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-(-1)-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(2+1-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-1)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19bcc4ffc2aa1140b1e57307a09982eb847f0e0440c73246c71cb4d3a58c9ce9"} {"question" "q-109644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}"] ["math" "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}"] ["math" "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{t}^{3}{y}^{′}-5ty=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{t}^{3}{y}^{′}+5ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+5t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=5t{x}_{1}+4{t}^{3}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074007/state.json" "file_sha256" "6e6759837e1add5d34f1ec433eb65640bacb6e9408f56cade081a3b89f3b7848"} {"question" "q-109691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/ma